{
 "_about": {
  "purpose": "Tribal labels read off map faces, with the scan pixel each one sits at, and the control points that put those pixels on the ground.",
  "method": [
   "The map face was cut into overlapping tiles and every tile read at full scan",
   "resolution. A label is recorded when its text is legible; its pixel is the",
   "centre of the label as read, not the centroid of the territory, because the",
   "territory has no drawn boundary on any of these sheets - the name is spread",
   "across it in letterspaced capitals and that is the whole of the annotation.",
   "",
   "So a coordinate here says 'the engraver centred this tribe's name here'. Six",
   "labels measured on the tiles run 175 to 400 px, 14 to 32 km, so that is a",
   "statement about the tribe's location to within a tribe's width and nothing",
   "finer. Reading the same label twice from different tiles agreed to 3-5 px",
   "(0.3 km), so the reading is not the limit; the annotation is."
  ],
  "control_points": [
   "Towns, not the printed graticule. The graticule is there and readable, but the",
   "sheet is scanned with a slight rotation and its own frame is not square: the",
   "8 degree tick on the top border and the 8 degree tick on the bottom border are",
   "141 px apart in x. Towns whose modern coordinates are known settle the",
   "transform and, in the residual, measure how far the compilation itself is out.",
   "Each town pixel is the centre of its printed symbol, read the same way as a",
   "label."
  ],
  "reading_confidence": "high = read without hesitation at full resolution; medium = the letters are broken or the name is unfamiliar and the transcription could be wrong by a letter or two."
 },
